Returning to stash every 20,000 moves = once in a game or twice in a
really long one. For me, that is. (Average game length of last 40
ascensions: 31988.5 moves)
 
> If more people agree, I think we may agree that it can easily be
> abolished.

I'm not one of them. Sure, invisibility is not something that you want
to cast in every game or even with all wizards, but occasionally you
come into a situation where you are able to cast invisibility in a
situation where you want to be invisible and you don't have the ring
or a potion, if only for the reason that then you can get permanent
invisibility from a stalker.

> I consider healing the exclusive domain of "praying" or "meditating"
> classes, and less of a spell, but more a prayer. May be my D&D hobby.

It is your D&D hobby. Casting healing spells was the traditional main
source of income for real life wizards while the other source was
finding lost or stolen items. (And those who wanted to risk their
lives also made a lot of money by cursing people. For a long time it
used to be that practicing white magic was legal and only black magic
got you executed. This changed when the diabolic origin of all magic
made its way into the legal theory). Hmm. perhaps I should play safe
and clarify that I didn't write "successfully casting" above and that
by "wizard" I mean any person who was more or less professional magic
user even though some people want to restrict that term to mean only
those few academically trained scholars who dabbled in dark arts.

The only spells that I don't remember ever casting are restore
ability, jumping, create familiar, and turn undead. Detect food, cure
blindness, clairvoyance, invisibility, and protection have very
limited uses but come handy occasionally. I find all other spells
useful.


